course: everyday
lesson: 368
title: Speaking up is bravery too
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In assembly the head teacher asks: «Who saw how the window by the bike shed got broken?» Three hundred children stare at their shoes. One hand goes up — Sam's. His face goes the colour of a tomato and his voice wobbles, but he says what he saw. Afterwards nobody calls him Scaredy-Sam for a whole day.
explain: Speaking up gets easier with practice. When Jake grabs little Ollie's football, Sam says, in front of everyone: «Leave Ollie alone, he's only in Year 3.» His knees shake. Jake is so surprised he actually gives the ball back. Lily tells the dinner lady the big boys keep taking the Year 3s' crisps, and Sam drops a note in the class worry box: «Someone is getting hit in the boys' toilets.»
explain: Then Sam asks Mrs Okafor, in front of the whole class: «Why do boys who fight get a detention, but girls who fight just get a talking-to?» The class goes silent. Mrs Okafor thinks, and says: «That's a fair question, Sam. Fighting is fighting. I'll talk to the head.» Maddie, who has had plenty of «talking-tos», looks at Sam as if he had grown a second head.
explain: Maddie has her own moment. She stands up and says the Station Road boy did not start the fight — her own friend Kieran did. Jake tells the head the real reason he hit someone: the boy said something nasty about his mum, who is in hospital. That is not an excuse, but it helps the grown-ups help him. Lily tells her friends she does not find the ponytail tugging cute — she finds it annoying. And Sam, braver now, tells Maddie she should not have pulled Chloe's plaits. Maddie scowls — and the next day she says sorry to Chloe.
fact: Speaking up for what is true or fair is a kind of courage, just like standing up to danger.
fact: Feeling nervous, blushing or shaking when you speak up is normal and does not make it any less brave.
fact: Telling a grown-up when younger children are being picked on helps protect them.
fact: Many schools have a worry box or a trusted adult so children can report problems safely, even without their name.
fact: Telling the truth about who started a fight is fair, even when it is your own friend.
fact: Rules should be the same for boys and girls: fighting is fighting.
fact: Explaining why you did something wrong does not make it right, but it helps adults understand and help.
fact: You can say clearly that you don't like being touched or teased, even if others call it «just a joke».
fact: Telling a friend kindly that they did something wrong is hard, but real friends do it.
fact: The more often someone speaks up, the easier it usually gets.
exercise: In assembly, why was putting his hand up brave for Sam? | He was scared but told the truth in front of everyone | Because he had broken the window himself and wanted to own up | Because nobody was watching him | Because he wanted to miss lessons
exercise: Sam went red and his voice wobbled. Did that make him less brave? | No, being nervous and doing it anyway is brave | Yes, brave people never blush or go red | Yes, his voice should be steady | No, because he wasn't really nervous at all, he was just cold
exercise: Why did Sam tell Jake to leave Ollie alone? | Ollie was much younger and couldn't stand up for himself | Sam wanted the football for himself | Ollie had asked Sam to go round and fight Jake for him at the gate | Sam wanted to start a fight with Jake
exercise: How did Sam stand up for Ollie without fighting? | He used clear words, not his fists | He pushed Jake over on the grass first | He threw Ollie's ball hard at Jake's head | He fetched his big brother to hit Jake
exercise: Why did Lily tell the dinner lady about the crisps? | So a grown-up could stop the big boys taking them | To get the crisps for herself | To get the Year 3s into trouble with the dinner lady | Because she wanted to be a dinner lady
exercise: What is the worry box for? | Telling a teacher about problems safely, even without your name | Keeping lost property safe | Posting letters to Father Christmas | Collecting money from every class for the school trips and outings
exercise: Why was Sam's note about the toilets important? | Someone was being hurt and adults needed to know | It was a joke to scare the teachers and get them running | So the toilets would be cleaned | So Sam could get a sticker
exercise: What did Sam's question to Mrs Okafor point out? | Boys and girls were punished differently for the same thing | Girls never fight at all, so it was only the boys' fault | Boys should get no punishment | Detentions are always unfair
exercise: Why was Maddie's speaking up so hard for her? | She had to tell the truth about her own friend | She didn't know who started the fight | She was very scared of the teacher and her loud voice | She was the one who started it
exercise: Jake said the boy was nasty about his mum in hospital. What does this explanation do? | It helps adults understand and help, but doesn't make hitting right | It means that hitting the boy was completely fine | It means the other boy should get all the blame for the whole fight in the playground | It means Jake should get a reward
exercise: What did Lily say about the ponytail tugging? | It's not cute, it's annoying | It's just a nice way to say hello | She wants it to happen more often | She doesn't mind as it's a joke
exercise: Why was Sam's telling Maddie about Chloe's plaits a kind of courage? | Telling a friend she did wrong risks her anger | Because Maddie is smaller than Sam and can't hit back | Because Chloe asked him to | Because Sam pulled the plaits too
exercise: What happened after Sam spoke to Maddie? | She said sorry to Chloe the next day | She pulled Chloe's plaits again | She stopped talking to Sam forever and found a new best friend | She punched Sam on the nose
exercise: What did Mrs Okafor say about Sam's question? | It was fair, and fighting is fighting | It was rude, and he should sit down and be quiet | Girls are allowed to fight | Boys always start fights
exercise: How did speaking up change for Sam over time? | It got easier the more he did it | It got harder and harder each time | It stopped making him nervous at once | It made everyone at school hate him
exercise: Which of these is NOT speaking up? | Staying quiet when you saw what happened | Telling a teacher what you saw | Writing a note for the worry box about what you saw | Telling a friend she was wrong
test: Head teacher: «Anyone see who smashed the window?» Total silence. What's the brave thing if you saw? | Put your hand up and say what you saw | Keep quiet, because nobody ever likes a grass | Blame someone you don't like | Say you saw nothing and leave
test: «oi Year 3, give us ur ball». You're in Year 5. What's a good move? | Say clearly «leave him alone» and tell a teacher | Punch the big kid in the face | Walk off, it's not your problem | Help take the ball off him as a laugh
test: «Sam, ur mental, Jake'll batter u for that». What's true about Sam's words to Jake? | They were brave and fair, said without fists | They were stupid; he should've hit Jake first | They were rude to Jake | They broke a school rule
test: «my legs were literally shaking when I said it». Does that make it less brave? | No, being scared and still saying it is brave | Yes, brave means no shaking at all | Yes, it only counts if you shout | No, because shaking means it was easy
test: big lads nicking Year 3s' crisps every day. Lily tells the dinner lady. Kieran: «snitch». Who's right? | Lily, she's protecting the little ones | Kieran, you should never tell on anybody | Nobody, because crisps don't really matter | The big lads, because it's only a few crisps
test: «Kieran started it, not the Station Road kid» — says Maddie, his mate. Why does that matter? | The truth matters even about your own friend | It doesn't matter, because friends always come first | It means Kieran can't be her friend | It means the Station Road kid is perfect
test: Teacher, a bit rattled: «Are you saying I'm unfair, Sam?» Best reply? | «No, Miss, I'm asking why the rules are different» | «Yeah, you're well unfair» | «Forget it, Miss, I didn't say anything, honestly, nothing at all» | «Maddie told me to say it»
test: «it's just a laugh, Lily, we only tug it cos we like u». What's Lily's best reply? | «I don't like it. Stop.» | «OK, tug it a bit harder then» | Say nothing and cry later | Pull their hair back hard
test: «He said my mum's gonna die so I hit him». What should the head teacher do? | Help Jake with his worry and still deal with the hitting | Let Jake off completely because of his mum | Punish only the other boy | Tell Jake his mum is fine
test: «Maddie, u shouldn't have pulled Chloe's plaits, that was mean». Is Sam a good friend here? | Yes, real friends tell you when you're wrong | No, a good friend always takes your side | No, it's none of his business what she does to Chloe | Yes, but only if Chloe pays him
test: You saw someone getting hit in the toilets but you're scared to say it out loud. What's a good option? | Write it in the worry box or tell a teacher privately | Do nothing, and maybe it'll stop on its own | Go and tell the bully you saw him | Post about it online
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the act of reporting a witnessed incident to the head teacher in the presence of the assembled school? | It constitutes a form of courage notwithstanding visible nervousness | It indicates chiefly a desire to obtain favour with the members of staff present | It is of no value unless performed without fear | It is an act of disloyalty towards other pupils
exam: Identify the principal function of a worry box in a primary school. | To enable pupils to report concerns safely to staff | To store articles of lost property | To collect pupils' contributions towards the cost of school excursions | To record the results of examinations
exam: Given that a pupil struck another after an unkind remark concerning his mother's illness, select the response consistent with the established facts. | The explanation assists understanding but does not justify the blow | The explanation renders the blow entirely justified | The pupil who made the remark bears sole responsibility for the blow | No consequence ought to follow in such circumstances
exam: Ascertain which conduct is most consistent with the principle that rules apply equally to all pupils. | That fighting incurs the same consequence for boys and for girls | That girls are exempt from sanctions for fighting | That boys alone are liable to detention | That sanctions depend upon the popularity of the pupil concerned
